# Morphit operator PGP public keys
#
# This file contains PGP public keys used by THIS Morphit
# operator (the one whose website you're loading this from)
# for:
#
#   1. Signing the warrant canary at /canary.txt
#   2. Signing release announcements posted to the @morphit-fees
#      Blurt account or this operator's chosen channel
#   3. Receiving end-to-end encrypted security disclosures
#      that aren't routed via Matrix
#
# To verify a Morphit canary:
#   1. Download this file: curl -O https://this-operator/pgp_keys.asc
#   2. Import into your keyring: gpg --import pgp_keys.asc
#   3. Verify the canary:
#        curl -O https://this-operator/canary.txt
#        gpg --verify canary.txt
#   4. Check that the key fingerprint shown by gpg matches what
#      this operator has broadcast on-chain via their
#      morphit_operator_register_v1 op (one of the validation
#      mechanisms — chain-broadcast fingerprint should match).
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# OPERATOR: This file is a placeholder.  Replace the body below
# with your actual PGP public key block(s) before going live.
# A canary signed by a key that's NOT in this file gives readers
# no way to verify the signature, so:
#
#   1. Generate a release/canary signing key (or use an existing
#      one):
#        gpg --quick-gen-key 'Morphit Operator <op@example.com>' \
#            ed25519 sign 5y
#
#   2. Export the public key:
#        gpg --armor --export <fingerprint> > pgp_keys.asc
#
#   3. Replace this file's content with the export.
#
#   4. Confirm the fingerprint matches the one referenced in
#      your morphit_operator_register_v1 chain op (operators may
#      include the fingerprint as an extension field; if not,
#      mention it in your operator-instance contact_url page).
#
#   5. Set MORPHIT_CANARY_PGP_KEY_ID to the same fingerprint and
#      run scripts/canary/generate.sh — the canary will be signed
#      by this key, and readers will be able to verify it.
#
# Multiple keys are allowed in this file (e.g., a release-signing
# key + a separate canary key + a per-operator-staff transitional
# key during a rotation).  Each verifier operates on the union.
#
# Keys SHOULD have a sensible expiration (5 years is a reasonable
# default).  Operators who plan to rotate keys before expiration
# can publish the new key here AND list a transition statement
# at the top of their canary so readers know to expect the
# rotation.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

